<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/fundamentals/package-validation/diagnostic-ids -->
<!--
  ApiCompat suppressions for Azure.ResourceManager.ProviderHub 1.3.0, measured against the 1.2.1
  baseline. The regeneration for API version 2025-10-01 produces 97 findings: 13 CP0001, 82 CP0002,
  1 CP0007 and 1 CP0008.

  CP0001 and CP0002 are suppressed at assembly level - a DiagnosticId with Left/Right and no Target
  suppresses every finding of that id for this assembly - rather than by listing each signature.

  This is deliberate and is not a convenience. An upstream compliance remediation removed a set of
  internal-only Microsoft identifiers from the public Microsoft.ProviderHub specification, so the
  corresponding API surface is no longer generated. 26 of the CP0001/CP0002 findings name one of
  those identifiers. Because ApiCompat targets are fully qualified signatures, enumerating them here
  would reproduce in this repository the exact identifiers that change exists to remove. The
  removals are described by location and shape in the package CHANGELOG.md instead.

  The scope of the suppression is bounded:

    - It applies only to this assembly. Other packages are unaffected.
    - It applies only to CP0001 and CP0002. Every other ApiCompat rule still runs and still fails
      the build, which is why the two findings below are recorded individually.
    - ApiCompat itself still runs. ApiCompatVersion remains set in the project file, so the
      ValidateApiCompatVersionPresent guard is satisfied and this package is not listed in
      ApiCompatVersionOptOut.txt.

  The trade-off is that a future unrelated CP0001 or CP0002 break in this package would also be
  suppressed until these entries are removed. They should be removed at the next GA release, when
  ApiCompatVersion advances to 1.3.0 and the 1.2.x comparison no longer applies;
  eng/scripts/Remove-ObsoleteApiCompatBaselines.ps1 handles that cleanup.

  Not every CP0001/CP0002 finding is compliance-related. The others come from deliberate modelling
  changes in 2025-10-01 and are described in the package CHANGELOG.md:

    - The resource-deletion-policy rename, which the specification records with @renamedFrom on
      API version 2025-10-01, so it is a service decision rather than an artifact of regeneration.
    - The promotion of OperationsPutContent to an ARM resource. The specification declares it
      Azure.ResourceManager.ProxyResource, so the flat model exposed by 1.2.x was the anomaly and
      reverting it would regress ARM conformance.
    - Collection properties widened from IReadOnlyList<T> to IList<T>, and model factory overloads
      that existed only to match the shape of the 1.2.x contract.

  Four findings that would otherwise appear here were fixed rather than suppressed, by restoring the
  members in src/Custom with signatures identical to 1.2.1: the synchronous
  ProviderRegistrationResource.Delete pair, and the flattened
  ResourceTypeRegistrationResourceManagementOptions.BatchProvisioningSupportSupportedOperations
  property.
-->
<Suppressions xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <!-- Assembly-level: see header. Names no removed identifier. -->
  <Suppression>
    <DiagnosticId>CP0001</DiagnosticId>
    <Left>lib/netstandard2.0/Azure.ResourceManager.ProviderHub.dll</Left>
    <Right>lib/netstandard2.0/Azure.ResourceManager.ProviderHub.dll</Right>
  </Suppression>
  <Suppression>
    <DiagnosticId>CP0002</DiagnosticId>
    <Left>lib/netstandard2.0/Azure.ResourceManager.ProviderHub.dll</Left>
    <Right>lib/netstandard2.0/Azure.ResourceManager.ProviderHub.dll</Right>
  </Suppression>
  <!--
    ResourceAccessPolicy was converted from a closed enum to an extensible enum so that values the
    service still returns round-trip as strings instead of failing to deserialize. The type name is
    not itself affected by the compliance change, so these two are recorded individually.
  -->
  <Suppression>
    <DiagnosticId>CP0007</DiagnosticId>
    <Target>T:Azure.ResourceManager.ProviderHub.Models.ResourceAccessPolicy</Target>
  </Suppression>
  <Suppression>
    <DiagnosticId>CP0008</DiagnosticId>
    <Target>T:Azure.ResourceManager.ProviderHub.Models.ResourceAccessPolicy</Target>
  </Suppression>
</Suppressions>
